ential Medical Triage Practices\nL109: \nL110: By leveraging advanced AI models, teams at the University of Pennsylvania are aiming to bring cutting-edge automation to emergency response.\nL111: \nL112: December 18, 2025\nL113: \nL114: cite25†Learn More L115: \nL116: Blog\nL117: \nL118: February 09, 2026\nL119: \nL120: #### Computer Vision\nL121: \nL122: #### Open Source\nL123: #### Reducing Government Costs and Increasing Access to Greenspaces in the United Kingdom with DINO\nL124: \nL125: Meta's DINOv2 model is enhancing reforestation efforts around the world. Learn how the UK government is using DINO to help reduce costs and increase access to greenspaces.\nL126: \nL127: February 09, 2026\nL128: \nL129: cite26†Learn More L130: \nL131: Blog\nL132: \nL133: February 20, 2025\nL134: \nL135: #### Open Source\nL136: #### How Orakl Oncology is using DINOv2 to accelerate cancer treatment discovery\nL137: \nL138: Orakl Oncology aims to accelerate cancer research and drug development by combining experimental, lab-based insights and machine learning.\nL139: \nL140: February 20, 2025\nL141: \nL142: cite27†Learn More L143: \nL144: Blog\nL145: \nL146: December 18, 2025\nL147: \nL148: #### Computer Vision\nL149: #### The Universities Space Research Association Applies Segment Anything Model for Responding to Flood Emergencies\nL150: \nL151: The Universities Space Research Association and Meta are collaborating to help support water observing systems set up by the U.S. Geological Survey.\nL152: \nL153: December 18, 2025\nL154: \nL155: cite28†Learn More L156: \nL157: Blog\nL158: \nL159: December 16, 2025\nL160: \nL161: #### Computer Vision\nL162: #### Introducing SAM Audio: The First Unified Multimodal Model for Audio Separation\nL163: \nL164: SAM Audio transforms audio processing by making it easy to isolate any sound from complex audio mixtures using natural, multimodal prompts — whether through text, visual cues, or marking time segments.\nL165: \nL166: December 16, 2025\nL167: \nL168: cite29†Learn More L169: \nL170: Blog\nL171: \nL172: November 19, 2025\nL173: \nL174: #### Computer Vision\nL175: #### Introducing SAM 3D: Powerful 3D Reconstruction for Physical World Images\nL176: \nL177: This release introduces two new state-of-the-art models: SAM 3D Objects for object and scene reconstruction, and SAM 3D Body for human body and shape estimation.\nL178: \nL179: November 19, 2025\nL180: \nL181: cite30†Learn More L182: \nL183: Blog\nL184: \nL185: November 24, 2025\nL186: \nL187: #### Computer Vision\nL188: #### How Conservation X Labs Is Using Segment Anything Model 3 for Endangered Wildlife Monitoring\nL189: \nL190: Start-ups like Conservation X Labs are using Meta’s Segment Anything Models to bolster on-the-ground conservation expertise.\nL191: \nL192: November 24, 2025\nL193: \nL194: cite31†Learn More L195: \nL196: Blog\nL197: \nL198: November 21, 2025\nL199: \nL200: #### Open Source\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAvailability of submissions - arXiv info (https://info.arxiv.org/help/availability.html)\nciteturn27186view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://info.arxiv.org/help/availability.html\",\"lineno\":160}); Total lines: 206\nL115: Brand\nL116:     * cite67†Name and logo use L117:     * cite68†Brand Pillars L118:     * cite69†Colors L119:     * cite70†Fonts L120:     * cite71†Logos L121:     * cite72†Quotes L122:     * cite73†Branded Merchandise L123:     * cite74†Tagline L124:     * cite75†The arXiv Voice L125:   * [Input]\nL126: \nL127: cite76†Labs L128: \nL129: Labs\nL130:     * cite77†arXivLabs: Showcase L131:   * [Input]\nL132: \nL133: cite78†Policies L134: Policies\nL135:     * cite79†Code of conduct L136:     * cite80†Privacy policy L137:     * cite81†Identity, affiliation, and registration L138:     * cite82†Submittal agreement L139:     * cite5